# UI Accessibility + Safety Standards

These rules are mandatory for every UI surface in this clinical
decision-support application. They sit on top of
`design-system-ui.mdc` (which covers visual conventions) and
`ui-testing.mdc` (which covers how to verify them).

## 1. Destructive actions are gated, always

Every server-side destructive action (delete, retire, dismiss,
reject, reset, finalise, publish, force-delete) MUST be behind an
explicit confirmation dialog. Never fire a destructive API on a bare
`onClick`, never use `window.confirm()` or `window.prompt()`.

Two patterns are approved:

### a. ConfirmDialog (single-step deletes)

`services/ui/src/components/ConfirmDialog.tsx` for short, low-risk
confirms.

```tsx
const [confirmDelete, setConfirmDelete] = useState<string | null>(null);
<IconButton
  aria-label={`Remove ${term}`}
  onClick={() => setConfirmDelete(id)}
>
  <DeleteIcon />
</IconButton>
{confirmDelete && (
  <ConfirmDialog
    title="Remove clinical code?"
    body={`This will permanently delete ${term} (${confirmDelete}).`}
    confirmLabel="Remove"
    variant="danger"
    onConfirm={() => { handleRemove(confirmDelete); setConfirmDelete(null); }}
    onCancel={() => setConfirmDelete(null)}
  />
)}
```

### b. Type-the-name confirm gate (irreversible / runtime-impact)

For deletes that take a runtime asset offline (active pathways,
active prompts, active workflows), the confirm button MUST stay
disabled until the user types the asset name verbatim. The canonical
implementation lives in
`services/ui/src/app/admin/pathways/dialogs.tsx` ->
`DeletePathwayDialog`.

```tsx
<Button
  variant="contained"
  color="error"
  disabled={confirmName.trim() !== target.name}
  onClick={onConfirm}
>
  Delete pathway
</Button>
```

Use this pattern wherever the asset has a runtime side effect (Plan
Definitions, prompt sets, model configs, workflow definitions).

## 2. Single primary action per page

`ToolbarActions` MUST contain at most one entry with `variant:
"primary"`. Destructive actions (Delete, Retire, Reset) MUST never be
the page-level primary -- demote them to per-row secondary actions.
This pattern reduces accidental destructive clicks; it is also
verified by static-source VTs.

## 3. Keyboard discipline

### Destructive keyboard shortcuts

Reject / dismiss / delete shortcuts MUST guard against text-input
focus AND show a confirmation dialog before executing. Do not use the
`Delete` key alone -- always pair with a modifier (Cmd/Ctrl).

```tsx
function isTypingTarget(t: EventTarget | null): boolean {
  if (!(t instanceof HTMLElement)) return false;
  return (
    t instanceof HTMLInputElement ||
    t instanceof HTMLTextAreaElement ||
    t instanceof HTMLSelectElement ||
    t.isContentEditable ||
    Boolean(t.closest('[role="combobox"]'))
  );
}
```

### Focus management

- Every interactive element MUST be reachable by `Tab` and operable
  by `Enter` / `Space`.
- Open dialog -> trap focus; close dialog -> restore focus to the
  trigger.
- `Escape` closes any non-irreversible dialog. Type-the-name confirm
  gates may stay open (so an accidental `Escape` doesn't reopen the
  whole confirm flow).

## 4. Semantic structure

- Every page has exactly one `<h1>`. Use `Typography variant="h5"
  component="h1"` if the visual hierarchy needs a smaller heading.
  `StatHero` does NOT render an `<h1>` -- pages still need their own.
- Heading variants only on real headings -- never on sidebar brand,
  not on chips, not on labels.
- Card titles are `<h2>` or `<h3>` depending on nesting. Use
  `component="h2"` / `component="h3"` on the underlying Typography.
- Lists of items use `<ul>` / `<ol>` semantics where appropriate
  (MUI `List` does this for you).

## 5. Labels

Every input has a programmatic label.

- `TextField`: `label` prop. For search fields, `aria-label="Search
  ..."` plus a visible placeholder is acceptable.
- `IconButton`: ALWAYS `aria-label`.
- `CircularProgress`: ALWAYS `aria-label="Loading"` + `role="status"`.
- `SegmentedTabs`: ALWAYS pass `aria-label="<context>"` so screen
  readers can describe the filter group.
- `ToolbarActions`: button labels come from `action.label` -- never
  ship an icon-only `ToolbarAction`.

## 6. Colour contrast

All text on white must meet WCAG 2.1 AA (4.5:1 for body, 3:1 for
large / bold text). Banned on white backgrounds:

- `#94a3b8` (2.4:1)
- `#e2e8f0` (1.2:1)
- `rgba(0,0,0,0.38)` (2.7:1, MUI `text.disabled` on light theme)

The minimum caption colour is `#6B7280`. Tone chips use the canonical
knowledge palette (NICE blue `#1E40AF` on `#DBEAFE`; platform slate
`#475569` on `#F1F5F9`; LEO domain green `#047857` on `#ECFDF5`) --
all of which clear AA.

When in doubt, use a token from `roles` rather than hardcoding hex.

## 7. Empty / loading / no-match states

Never `return null` when data is absent. Every page has three
reachable states: loading, empty (no data at all), and no-match
(filter returned nothing).

```tsx
if (loading) return <SkeletonList />;
if (!plans.length) return (
  <Alert severity="info">
    No pathways configured yet. <Button onClick={onNew}>Create the first one</Button>
  </Alert>
);
if (!visible.length) return (
  <Alert severity="info">No pathways match this filter.</Alert>
);
```

The copy strings MUST be the same as in the corresponding VT
(`pathways-list-chrome.test.tsx` is the reference).

## 8. Error handling

Never swallow API errors. Every fetch surfaces failures to the user.

```tsx
// BAD
const data = await fetchData().catch(() => []);

// GOOD
try {
  setData(await fetchData());
} catch (err) {
  setSectionError(plainEnglishError(err, "Unable to load pathways. Please retry."));
}
```

For raw `fetch()` calls, always check `res.ok` before treating as
success. Surface 4xx / 5xx separately from network failures so the
user knows whether to retry or whether something is wrong with the
request.

## 9. Native dialogs are banned

Never use `alert()`, `window.confirm()`, or `window.prompt()`. Use
MUI `Alert` / `Snackbar` for feedback and `ConfirmDialog` (or the
type-the-name pattern) for confirmations.

## 10. Reduced motion

All framer-motion usage MUST honour `prefers-reduced-motion`. Use
`useMotion()` from `@leo/design-system`, which collapses transition
durations to 0ms when the user has reduced motion enabled. Never
animate critical content (alerts, errors, confirmations) -- they
must appear instantly.

## 11. Density

The compact density toggle (Phase 1 cont) reduces vertical padding;
it MUST NOT reduce target sizes below WCAG 2.5.5 minimums (24px x
24px AA, 44px x 44px AAA target). When implementing density support
in a primitive, only adjust spacing -- never shrink interactive
targets.

## 12. Health literacy

All patient- or non-specialist-facing text MUST follow
`health-literacy.mdc` (reading age 9-11, plain English, short
sentences, active voice). Internal admin / QA copy may use clinical
terms but should still be unambiguous.
